					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-4449201">
																		It&#039;s line-by-line time
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2902864&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200994" alt="MaliceAforethought" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2902864)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;" >MaliceAforethought</a></span> <span class="odate time_1576676672 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Dec 2019 13:44</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4449201">
									

<p>I love this conceptually, but the more I think about it the more things stand out at me and stop me from upvoting.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Show&nbsp;critique</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;this</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4872 is to be fed a diet of small flies and other typical arachnid prey.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm fairly sure the prey of arachnids vary wildly by species. This reads like somebody didn't want to look up what this spider actually ate. At the very least, make something up &#8212; if someone more knowledgeable corrects you on it, then they've done the research for you anyway!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The population of this chamber is never to exceed 50.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I get that this is implied to be &quot;fifty SCP-4872 specimens&quot;, but it's never specified. What if there's a live food-bug in there &#8212; do they have to kill a spider to make room for it?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Interaction with SCP-4872 is not to take place without wearing a padded suit at least six centimeters thick and without any opening greater than one centimeter in size.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The way you've ordered the subjects of this sentence mean &quot;Don't interact without two things. 1) This special padded suit. 2) An opening greater than one centimetre in size&quot;. I'm fairly sure that's the exact opposite of what you wanted to say.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Biweekly inspection of SCP-4872's containment cell is to take place</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A minor thing, feel free to disregard, but &quot;SCP-4872's containment cell is to be inspected biweekly&quot; seems like the far more natural way of phrasing this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Although SCP-4872 are capable of sustaining themselves <strong>from small bugs</strong>, their preferred prey are humans.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a good line. Not as &quot;oh shit&quot; as some other articles, but it sets up the stakes well pretty early in the text. I do think it should be &quot;sustaining themselves <em>on</em> small bugs&quot;, however.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the venom of SCP-4872 has <strong>anesthesiac</strong> properties</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The word you're looking for is either &quot;anaesthetic&quot;, &quot;analgesic&quot;, or just &quot;numbing&quot;. An anaesthesiac would really just be&#8230; somebody with anaesthesia.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>which can cause numbness at the point of injection</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Another good line. Made me physically uncomfortable as I realised what these spiders were doing to people.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>such as schools</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd personally give a couple more examples, with schools as the rule-of-three stinger. That's just me, though.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This will cause the subject to experience a &quot;continued&quot; version of their life while asleep.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This gives the game away <em>far</em> too early.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the larvae will leave its host through the host's ears</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Gross, but why not the nose, mouth, and eyes as well? Or the incision they entered through?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Brain activity is believed to be used as a form of sustenance by SCP-4872 and its larvae</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is some <em>wild</em> speculation from Researchers. The reader can draw the conclusion from the second half of the sentence anyway.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Addendum 4872-1:</strong> Affected Subject Interview</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This whole section was <em>by far</em> the worst-written part of the article. It felt stilted, flat, impersonal, and overly contrived to give a pretty naff expository dump on an otherwise solid piece. I dunno what I'd do to convey the same information, but it wouldn't be poor little Jimmy's first kiss.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For instance, a 50% match to an Alabama seamstress, a 40% match to a Philadelphia criminal case lawyer, a 43% match to a Philadelphia dog trainer, and a 12% match to the current mayor of Philadelphia.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a good detail, but &#8212; and I'm not a biologist &#8212; I'm <em>99% sure that's not how DNA works</em>. I think you can get matches with x% certainty, and I think that would be a reasonable thing to put here? But I'm also reasonably certain that any differences between people's DNA, given that they're the same species, would be around 0.1%. Like, I think we share about half of our genes with bananas, and that seamstress doesn't strike me as a fruit. This is, of course, based on half-remembered biology lessons and wikipedia, so please don't crucify me if I'm wrong; it just struck me as contrary to how I thought things worked.</p>
<p>And, finally, one more note &#8212; I really wish these weren't spiders. I get that they're a good fit, and people are already scared of them which makes for an initial boost to the horror factor &#8212; they're just so <em>overused</em>. There are a lot more interesting creepy-crawlies out there, and most of 'em haven't had SCP articles yet. Spiders, on the other hand, already have 32 articles, and they lessen the memorability of any specific scip substantially in my eyes.</p>
</div>
